            <ArticleTitle>Whole exome sequencing in inborn errors of immunity: use the power but mind the limits.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>421-430</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1097/ACI.0000000000000398</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="PURPOSE OF REVIEW" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">Next-generation sequencing, especially whole exome sequencing (WES), has revolutionized the molecular diagnosis of inborn errors of immunity. This review summarizes the generation and analysis of next-generation sequencing data.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RECENT FINDINGS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">The focus is on prioritizing strategies for unveiling the potential disease-causing variant. We also highlighted oversights and imperfections of WES and targeted panel sequencing, as well as the need for functional validation.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SUMMARY" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">The information is crucial for a judicious use of WES by researchers, but even more so by the clinical immunologist.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
